INSERT INTO lr_explanation_steps (explanation_id, position, label, body) SELECT e.id, v.position, v.label, v.body FROM lr_explanations e JOIN lr_questions q ON q.id=e.question_id CROSS JOIN (VALUES (0, $pilot$Conclusion (so)$pilot$, $pilot$A lack of funds for research adversely affects the degree to which a university can fulfill its central mission.
  *rephrase:* A lack of research funding hurts how well a university can educate.$pilot$), (1, $pilot$Evidence$pilot$, $pilot$- A university's primary task is to educate.
  - To teach well, professors have to keep up with new developments, which requires research.
  - Many universities can't afford to support research adequately.$pilot$), (2, $pilot$Evaluate$pilot$, $pilot$On Main Conclusion questions, the conclusion often sits in the first sentence or right after a pivot, not at the end. So a final "So ..." sentence deserves a check before we trust it.

Here, it holds up. Run the Support Test: *why should we believe a lack of research funding hurts a university's central mission?* Because the mission is to educate, teaching well requires research, and many universities can't fund research. Every earlier sentence feeds into the last one, and nothing comes after it.

Also notice the referential language. "Its central mission" only makes sense if we go back to the first sentence: the mission is to educate.$pilot$), (3, $pilot$Goal$pilot$, $pilot$Swap the reference for the specific idea, then look for the closest match: *a lack of research funding hurts how well a university can educate.*$pilot$)) v(position,label,body) WHERE q.qid=$pilot$PT128-S3-Q16$pilot$ AND e.style='single' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lr_explanation_steps s WHERE s.explanation_id=e.id) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lr_explanation_choice_notes (explanation_id, letter, body) SELECT e.id, v.letter, v.body FROM lr_explanations e JOIN lr_questions q ON q.id=e.question_id CROSS JOIN (VALUES ($pilot$A$pilot$, $pilot$The author never says this in one sentence. It's what you get by joining two pieces of the second sentence: teaching well requires keeping up with new developments, and keeping up requires research. So (A) follows from the premises, but it's a step on the way. Its job is to support the last sentence, which is about what a lack of funding does to a university. (A) doesn't mention funding at all.$pilot$), ($pilot$B$pilot$, $pilot$"The root of ineffective teaching" makes lack of funding *the* cause of bad teaching. The author only says it *adversely affects* education, which leaves room for plenty of other causes.$pilot$), ($pilot$C$pilot$, $pilot$This is the first sentence, reworded (and nudged from "educate" to "effective teaching"). It's there to tell us what "central mission" means. It supports the conclusion; it isn't it.$pilot$), ($pilot$D$pilot$, $pilot$Line it up:
- "Lack of funds for research": a lack of funds for research.
- "reduces the quality of education a university provides": adversely affects how well a university fulfills its central mission (educating).

The only work required is swapping "central mission" for "education," which is exactly what the Goal step set up.$pilot$), ($pilot$E$pilot$, $pilot$The author never proposes a fix. She only says underfunding hurts education. Calling for new funding sources is a What Next answer: something the author might say next, but didn't.$pilot$)) v(letter,body) WHERE q.qid=$pilot$PT128-S3-Q16$pilot$ AND e.style='single' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lr_explanation_choice_notes n WHERE n.explanation_id=e.id) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

